   "title": "How to use · v1.0.2",
   "widgets_values": [
    "## How to use · v1.0.2\n\n**What it does:** edits one picture with the MOSS-Image edit LoRA. You say what to change; the rest should stay. Core ComfyUI nodes only.\n\n1. **Your inputs** (green group):\n   - **Your picture**: any size; it is scaled to about 1 megapixel and the result has that size.\n   - **What to change**: one short instruction. Say what to keep as well. The saved text is written for `example.png` (the drawing that ships with ComfyUI); write your own for your picture.\n   - **Steps** 30, **CFG** 4: the template's. **Seed**: fixed; a new number gives another try.\n2. **Models**: four files, see the Downloads note.\n3. **Picture + instruction**: nothing to change.\n4. **Sampling**: euler / normal, Skip Layer Guidance on block 22 for the whole run (template).\n5. **Run**. The result is saved to `output/MOSS-Image/`; the slider node shows before and after.\n\n**What to expect:** Same size as your picture at about 1 MP (1248x832 for a 3:2 photo), 44 to 45 s on the lab's 24 GB card in a 10 GiB budget.\n**Weak spot:** in my tests the edit often changed more than asked: a hat swap also turned the woman's head (2 of 2 seeds), a forest-to-winter instruction drew a new winter scene instead of the same trees (3 of 3 runs), and the saved instruction on example.png gave a half yellow vest (seed 42) or no change (seed 43). Try several seeds.\n\n**Tested on:** ComfyUI 0.45.3 on the lab server (RTX PRO 4000 Blackwell 24 GB), --reserve-vram 13.5 --disable-dynamic-vram, no custom nodes. Not run on a 12 GB card yet.\n"
   ]
  },
